{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And it shall be when YHWH brings you.</b> And likewise the adjacent parashah begins ״והיה כי יביאך ה׳״ a second time. The first יביאך is plene with a yod, alluding to the first entry under Joshua, who fought ten battles — and these are: Jericho, Ai, Gibeon, Makkedah, Libnah, Lachish, Eglon, Hebron, Debir, and Waters of Merom. And concerning it Scripture says <b>which He swore to your fathers,</b> for the oath was to the fathers, in the manner of what the prophet said (Micah 7:20): ״אשר נשבעת לאבותינו מימי קדם״. And the second one is defective in its yod, alluding to the ingathering of the exiles at the future time, when no war will be needed there; and concerning it Scripture says ״אשר נשבע לך ולאבותיך״ — regarding the promises and the wondrous consolations that the prophets prophesied to us after the fathers. And concerning it Scripture says ״ונתנה לך״, for then the Land will be ours as an everlasting gift, never again to be destroyed — something that was not so at any time in the past. And what Scripture says, <b>unto the land of the Canaanite,</b> — it mentions the Land of Israel by the name 'the Canaanite' because he was a cursed slave, and it is impossible for him to hold possession of it, for a slave cannot hold possession of the master's property; therefore it was delivered to him and not to any other nation, like a king who delivers his property into the hand of his slaves to give it to his son when he grows up."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {0 "Place names in the list of Joshua's ten battles transcribed as they appear in the Hebrew; rabbinic forms (e.g. עגלונה, חברונה) rendered with their standard biblical equivalents (Eglon, Hebron)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:58:34.429629441Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}